<article class="prose">
  <h1>What this is</h1>
  <p>
    <strong>rehash</strong> crawls a list of sites, rewrites the headlines it
    finds, and ranks the links the way reddit does — score against age, so a
    story has to keep earning its place on the front page.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the links come from</h2>
  <p>
    Sources live in <code>config/sources.yml</code>. Each one is either an
    RSS/Atom feed or a plain HTML page scraped with CSS selectors, and each one
    belongs to a community. Nothing about the crawler is news-specific: any page
    that lists links works.
  </p>
  <ul class="sources">
    <% Rehash.config.sources.each do |source| %>
      <li>
        <a href="<%= h source.url %>"><%= h source.name %></a>
        <span class="domain">(<%= h source.type %> → r/<%= h source.community %>)</span>
      </li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>

  <h2>How headlines get rewritten</h2>
  <p>
    Rewriting is pluggable — set <code>REWRITER</code> to pick one:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>rule_based</code> (default): offline and deterministic. Strips section
      labels, the trailing outlet name, shouting, hype clauses and stray punctuation.</li>
    <li><code>claude</code>: genuine rewrites through the Anthropic API. Needs
      <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>; falls back to the rule-based rewriter on any error.</li>
    <li><code>none</code>: keeps the publisher's headline verbatim.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every rewritten link keeps the original headline, one click away under the
    title — the rewrite is an editorial layer, not a replacement for the record.
  </p>

  <h2>Being a good citizen</h2>
  <p>
    The crawler identifies itself, honours <code>robots.txt</code>, waits between
    requests to the same host, stores only headline-length excerpts, and always
    links back to the publisher.
  </p>
</article>
